--TEST--
A bare limit name and its `[limits]` spelling are one key to every `Core\Config` member — `rule:config/ini-set-is-core-config-set`, `rule:config/three-changeability-classes`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:config/three-changeability-classes` writes one limit in five places — `[limits]`, `[limits.hard]`,
// `[app.limits]`, `[app.limits.hard]` and the bare name a `set` uses — so
// `Core\Config` resolves a dotless limit name in `[limits]`, the block the
// request-settable default is stated in. The four members share that one
// rule, which is what this case asks: a `set` through one spelling is a `get`
// through the other, and a `restore` through either undoes it.

// Nothing is in force yet: a case runs where there is no `nvs.toml`.
echo "before=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Config::all()), "\n";

Core\Config::set("memory", "64M");
echo "bare=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
echo "dotted=", Core\Config::get("limits.memory") ?? "unset", "\n";

// One key, so one entry — and it is filed under the dotted name the file
// would have written, not under the bare one the `set` used.
echo "all=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Config::all()), "\n";

// `restore` reads the name the same way, so the dotted spelling undoes the
// bare one's set.
Core\Config::restore("limits.memory");
echo "after=", Core\Config::get("memory") ?? "unset", "\n";
echo "left=", Core\Arr::count(Core\Config::all()), "\n";
--EXPECT--
before=0
bare=64M
dotted=64M
all=1
after=unset
left=0
